{
  "content_acceptable": false,
  "critical_error": true,
  "critical_evidence": [
    {
      "quote_or_cell": "Our walk-away point is any offer that gives under about 3 months of supply with no stock build",
      "why_wrong": "The document identifies no independent qualified supply alternative before months 5–6. Rejecting otherwise usable short-term supply solely because it falls below three months could bring forward a production stoppage. The stated BATNA does not justify this reservation point.",
      "correction": "Evaluate any bridge offer against the actual no-agreement outcome, including additional production preserved, commercial terms and liabilities. Continue localization and escalation while accepting beneficial partial coverage where appropriate; do not make three months an unconditional rejection threshold."
    },
    {
      "quote_or_cell": "Safety stock built early, e.g. 2-3 months of sets shipped ahead of the cut-off",
      "why_wrong": "Although framed as a proposal rather than an agreement, this central bridge-stock proposal is not reconciled with available capacity. Even assuming an immediate ramp to 2,500 sets/month and a 30-day planning month, 21 days yields about 1,750 sets. Serving ongoing demand consumes about 560, leaving at most about 1,190 incremental sets—less than the 1,600–2,400 sets proposed. No opening inventory is established. The day-21 fallback therefore relies on stock whose availability has not been demonstrated.",
      "correction": "Calculate net stock under both 1,500 and 2,500 sets/month, verify opening inventory and ramp feasibility, and explicitly quantify the remaining uncovered demand. Make extended production or verified existing stock a condition of the larger stock-build proposal, rather than treating it as achievable within the existing notice."
    }
  ],
  "omissions": [
    "An executable, capacity-constrained bridge plan connecting opening inventory, ongoing consumption, pre-cutoff output, agreed extension duration, tooling-release timing and coverage through local qualification.",
    "A no-extension contingency that quantifies the uncovered production period and identifies explicit escalation and production-allocation decisions. Freight and schedule changes alone do not replace unavailable lamps.",
    "A properly bounded conditional ZOPA: the document assumes a package 'should be acceptable to both sides' without establishing LPI's minimum acceptable terms, and its five-month minimum coverage is inconsistent with its three-month walk-away threshold."
  ],
  "evaluation_defect": false,
  "defect_reason": "",
  "rationale": "The submission correctly prioritizes the approved 21-day record over the draft twelve-week notice and does not invent an agreed extension. It provides sincere supplier engagement, relevant commercial levers, tooling-ownership considerations, diplomatic exit arrangements and a broadly appropriate parallel localization timeline. However, the central continuity plan lacks a feasible quantified stock-and-supply bridge, and its walk-away instruction could unnecessarily accelerate a production halt. These require substantive operational repair, not cosmetic changes."
}
